{"title":"2007 USAMO Problems/Problem 2","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solutions\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solutions</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>(<i>Gregory Galperin</i>) A <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Square\" title=\"Square\">square</a> grid on the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Cartesian_plane\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Cartesian plane\">Euclidean plane</a> consists of all <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Point\" title=\"Point\">points</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/a/7ca10ffe3531cf1faf6222be7eac407c47bef878.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(m,n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"51\" height=\"20\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> are <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Integer\" title=\"Integer\">integers</a>.  Is it possible to cover all grid points by an infinite family of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Circle\" title=\"Circle\">discs</a> with non-overlapping interiors if each disc in the family has <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Radius\" title=\"Radius\">radius</a> at least 5?\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solutions\">Solutions</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solutions\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p><b>Lemma.</b> Among 3 <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Tangent\" title=\"Tangent\">tangent</a> circles with radius greater than or equal to 5, one can always fit a circle with radius greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/8/9688e581d9cf8f2ac6b9d775b8e970f188e282f7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"26\" height=\"41\" /> between those 3 circles. \n</p><p><i>Proof.</i> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Descartes%27_Circle_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Descartes&#39; Circle Theorem (page does not exist)\">Descartes' Circle Theorem</a> states that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> is the curvature of a circle (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/3/2031d88479286b8a9b73e6140706b80ba784e2a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a=\\frac 1{r}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"45\" height=\"37\" />, positive for <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Externally_tangent\" title=\"Externally tangent\">externally tangent</a>, negative for <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Internally_tangent\" title=\"Internally tangent\">internally tangent</a>), then we have that \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/2/46217ef6c88907b22a3c8a7b392aff2f71b5a7c5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(a+b+c+d)^2=2(a^2+b^2+c^2+d^2)\\]\" width=\"298\" height=\"19\" />\nSolving for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, we get \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/5/055bcfa3d2b0d9ba159abd9cf9e3abfc2bffdab8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a=b+c+d+2 \\sqrt{bc+cd+db}\\]\" width=\"244\" height=\"18\" />\nTake the positive root, as the negative root corresponds to internally tangent circle.\n</p><p>Now clearly, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/7/8b74dc941a2088475daf03dcc8a74722f746dd6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b+c+d \\le \\frac 35$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"105\" height=\"37\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/7/ff7449d7bdecdf3c395e20ee37904fdc56908795.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$bc+cd+db\\le \\frac 3{25}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"139\" height=\"37\" />.\nSumming/<a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Square_root\" title=\"Square root\">square root</a>/multiplying appropriately shows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/7/ce7344b986ca0a5f18e8fd868ca6e3df5f9d1368.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a \\le \\frac{3 + 2 \\sqrt{3}}5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"101\" height=\"41\" />. Incidently, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/7/9776f99012b2c19e6b698806d3569ceca22f50e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3 + 2\\sqrt{3}}5 &lt; \\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"117\" height=\"41\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/d/a3d292d3616d175bc453a7a01e6292edc2cbb992.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a&lt; \\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"60\" height=\"21\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/3/1335b3c16c6728f20771b7d5a146cb443bb14b43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r &gt; \\frac 1{\\sqrt{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"59\" height=\"41\" />, as desired. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/b/3bbb9e25e5be095e317771a9c5d00877ee9c8012.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\blacksquare$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>For sake of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Contradiction\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Contradiction\">contradiction</a>, assume that we have a satisfactory placement of circles. Consider 3 circles, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/6/006f1f7ff3c4dae39c8b1de34e35643e2383e786.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p,\\ q,\\ r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"54\" height=\"11\" /> where there are no circles in between. By <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Appolonius%27_problem&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Appolonius&#39; problem (page does not exist)\">Appolonius' problem</a>, there exists a circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/d/e0d2bf360290fd61d1c1557e763f2622363b3d35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"13\" /> tangent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/6/006f1f7ff3c4dae39c8b1de34e35643e2383e786.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p,\\ q,\\ r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"54\" height=\"11\" /> externally that is between those 3 circles. Clearly, if we move <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/6/006f1f7ff3c4dae39c8b1de34e35643e2383e786.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p,\\ q,\\ r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"54\" height=\"11\" /> together, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/d/e0d2bf360290fd61d1c1557e763f2622363b3d35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"13\" /> must decrease in radius. Hence it is sufficient to consider 3 tangent circles. By lemma 1, there is always a circle of radius greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/8/9688e581d9cf8f2ac6b9d775b8e970f188e282f7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"26\" height=\"41\" /> that lies between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/6/006f1f7ff3c4dae39c8b1de34e35643e2383e786.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p,\\ q,\\ r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"54\" height=\"11\" />. However, any circle with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/d/d7da5b71577462d99fb6a40b93f68219fd3cfb8c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r&gt;\\frac 1{\\sqrt{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"59\" height=\"41\" /> must contain a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Lattice_point\" title=\"Lattice point\">lattice point</a>. (Consider placing an unit square parallel to the gridlines in the circle.) That is a contradiction. Hence no such tiling exists.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>It is not possible. The proof is by contradiction. Suppose that such a covering family <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/f/7df6156885f7ecb884a812ae7155a0ad3ea33684.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{F}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"15\" height=\"13\" /> exists. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/0/8a01c809af39448af06f112af201956788440fb4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D(P,\\rho)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"58\" height=\"18\" /> denote the disc with center <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/2/0027034d8a10372a06deaf4f4084c01956587479.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\rho$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"9\" height=\"11\" />. Start with an arbitrary disc <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/8/9f82c06d4be2d84de577bd56a9a336f24e99eaf7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D(O,r)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"18\" /> that does not overlap any member of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/f/7df6156885f7ecb884a812ae7155a0ad3ea33684.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{F}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"15\" height=\"13\" />. Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/8/9f82c06d4be2d84de577bd56a9a336f24e99eaf7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D(O,r)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"18\" /> covers no grid point. Take the disc <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/8/9f82c06d4be2d84de577bd56a9a336f24e99eaf7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D(O,r)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"18\" /> to be maximal in the sense that any further enlargement would cause it to violate the non-overlap condition. Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/8/9f82c06d4be2d84de577bd56a9a336f24e99eaf7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D(O,r)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"18\" /> is tangent to at least three discs in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/f/7df6156885f7ecb884a812ae7155a0ad3ea33684.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{F}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"15\" height=\"13\" />. Observe that there must be two of the three tangent discs, say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/4/0042175b943afbb647004fc546533e666a0b02c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D(A,a)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/7/c271a6d130160b9641edcac3bab7fa0e6ac2cdd0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D(B,b)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"18\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/c/25c384c026a9650df17626620716f6756c7d49ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle AOB\\leq 120^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"113\" height=\"15\" />. By the Law of Cosines applied to triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/0/890bd5d40a42153edcb992ad0a14cac52ca2eaf8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABO$\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" />,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/9/bc9be13b669a0e83a62b99dffe23433679a41720.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(a + b)^2\\leq (a + r)^2 + (b + r)^2 + (a + r)(b + r),\\]\" width=\"362\" height=\"19\" />\nwhich yields\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/1/471bed896eb892914fb1f08e21924f370f1cbf8b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[ab\\leq 3(a + b)r + 3r^2,\\]\" width=\"164\" height=\"19\" />\nand thus\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/b/b1be0563045d0b22c731fb64f95507d1e621ffbb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[12r^2\\geq (a - 3r)(b - 3r).\\]\" width=\"186\" height=\"19\" />\nNote that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/2/1924c4502e5bbfe82e1f73b50f0e4b09d5b9a1ff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r &lt; 1/\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"76\" height=\"21\" /> because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/8/9f82c06d4be2d84de577bd56a9a336f24e99eaf7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D(O,r)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"18\" /> covers no grid point, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/5/835573565a6a946b6e9e42910012c515af06e1cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a - 3r)(b - 3r)\\geq (5 - 3r)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"219\" height=\"19\" /> because each disc in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/f/7df6156885f7ecb884a812ae7155a0ad3ea33684.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{F}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"15\" height=\"13\" /> has radius at least 5. Hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/2/6f2b71fae0c8365b083b704568be39c500b92fe6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\sqrt{3}r\\geq 5 - 3r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"114\" height=\"19\" />, which gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/3/2034e12dff0cde616a029eb40b456ccf45bbde35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5\\leq (3 + 2\\sqrt{3})r &lt; (3 + 2\\sqrt{3})/\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"257\" height=\"21\" /> and thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/9/a393b5404f26dc34587858c3038a3a590650b157.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5\\sqrt{2} &lt; 3 + 2\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"124\" height=\"21\" />. Squaring both sides of this inequality yields <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/d/55d740ba67b4911de95beef6e9a783d85e36bafd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$50 &lt; 21 + 12\\sqrt{3} &lt; 21 + 12\\cdot 2 = 45$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"272\" height=\"18\" />. This contradiction completes the proof.\n</p><p><b>Remark:</b> The above argument shows that no covering family exists where each disc has radius greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/c/88cf813f0f651023e7308d685697402d5cd601d2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(3 + 2\\sqrt{3})/\\sqrt{2}\\approx 4.571$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"176\" height=\"21\" />. In the other direction, there exists a covering family in which each disc has radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/8/cc816a8f675c674c31a60dee835b4366e6051108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{13}/2\\approx 1.802$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"116\" height=\"21\" />. Take discs with this radius centered at points of the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/0/4a07a89885ed387db34649a1c25bbff3135faaf0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(2m + 4n + \\frac{1}{2}, 3m + \\frac{1}{2}\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"193\" height=\"43\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> are integers. Then any grid point is with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/2/e9285fe918a08c3bce27345d641b44c934272ca5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{13}/2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"51\" height=\"21\" /> of one of the centers and the distance between any two centers is at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/d/90dbd99bf5f9a37b7ca89b0bccecdf413e1c8afd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{13}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"18\" />. The extremal radius of a covering family is unknown.\n</p><p><i>Alternate solutions are always welcome. If you have a different, elegant solution to this problem, please <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit\">add it</a> to this page.</i>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li>&lt;url&gt;viewtopic.php?t=145844 Discussion on AoPS/MathLinks&lt;/url&gt;</li></ul>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO\" title=\"2007 USAMO\">2007 USAMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems\" title=\"2007 USAMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=27&amp;year=2007\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2007 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">Problem 1</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2007 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">Problem 3</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2007 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2007 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2007 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2007 USAMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_USAMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2007 USAMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017213958\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.056 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.079 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 350/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1663/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 48/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 10302/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   17.931      1 -total\n 37.26%    6.681      1 Template:USAMO_newbox\n 31.25%    5.603      1 Template:Alternate_solutions\n 29.55%    5.298      1 Template:MAA_Notice\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:4889:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017213958 and revision id 62795. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}